            <ArticleTitle>[Churg-Strauss syndrome and pulmonary fibrosis: an unusual association].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1259-62</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Churg-Strauss syndrome (CSS) is characterized by asthma, hypereosinophilia, and vasculitis involving at least two extrapulmonary organs.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE" NlmCategory="METHODS">We report a case of a patient with antineutrophilic cytoplasmic antibody-negative CSS who developed pulmonary interstitial fibrosis (PIF).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The possible relations between CSS and PIF are discussed. Because this case report is the first to describe features of pulmonary fibrosis in a patient with CSS, we cannot know whether this association is causal or fortuitous.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
